Stack and heap
Stack is usually the call stack, which is a specific data structure to allow recall of the program counter between method invocations
It is like a stack of vinyl records
Assume the bottommost record is main()
While enclosed methods are executing, the stack holds primitive variable references and the exact location at which the method was suspended
When “enclosed” methods finish their run, we resume the “ancestor” method from the exact instruction it was supposed to execute next
Size is 1 Megabyte by default in Java
This allows approximately 1000-10000 method calls to be suspended on top of one another
A typical non-recursive program will have less than 70 suspended methods
The java heap is NOT a heap structure; it is just “large memory”. Called the heap because objects are placed in arbitrary locations, as determined at the runtime

