Big-O (again)


Finding a number in an unsorted array:
T(n) = 3n + 3

O(n) is T(n) with all less significant terms stripped and all constants stripped

Then 
O(n) = n

Because, as n -> infinity, less powers of n and constants become insignificant

Definition:
	T(n) <= c*f(n) where T(n) is the exact count of computer steps, c is a constant f(n) is logn, n, n^2, ...
	
The above can be understood as T(n) is in the growth envelope of f(n) == logn or n or nlogn or n^2

All the logn algorithms are in the logn class

This class does logn work +/- some constants

The whole point of O(n) is that if you are in one class, you do less work than any algorithm of
the above classes as n approaches infinity

When n is a very large number, your large constant is irrelevant, because it's finite

In practical terms, if you calculate T(n), you can drop all lesser terms and all constants, and the
result is the class of the algorithm

A very practical outcomes is that we only care about loops when determining Big-O, and we don't
have to count every step meticulously

