Insertion Sort

Intuition:
Starting at index 1, insert the current element int its correct place among the previous indices

Example:

4 3 2 1

3 4 2 1

2 3 4 1

1 2 3 4

1 2 3 4


Code:
InsertionSort(int [] A)
n = A.length
for (i = 1; i < n; i++)
	target = A[i]
	j = i - 1
	while j >= 0 && target < A[j]
		A[j+1] = A[j]
		--j
	A[j+1] = target
	
Worst-case scenario: reverse sorted array: all indices have to propogate to the beginning.
No early termination of inner iteration. On average, n/2 per inner iteration.
O(n)*O(n/2) == O(n)*O(n) = O(n^2)

Best-case: sorted or nearly sorted array: Inner loop terminates immediately every time
O(n)*O(1) = O(n)


Comparison
1. both have the same wors-case performance: O(n^2)
2. selection sort is easier to comprehend/implement
3. Selection sort always operates at the worst case performance, while insertion sort can
terminate early for nearly sorted arrays

Of the two, insertion sort is a better algorithm, because real-world data is frequently
nearly sorted.

